In article <17406@cs.utexas.edu> varvel@cs.utexas.edu (Donald A. Varvel) writes:
>A mimic is a monster.  The *general* rule is that you remember
>where objects are after you leave a room, but that you don't
>"remember" where a monster is.  If you leave a shop that contains
>an active mimic, the mimic inactivates.  However, up until
>inactivation, it was moving.  You shouldn't be able to "remember"
>where it is, and you should have no idea what it will look like
>when you reenter the shop.  I therefore think it is entirely
>reasonable for mimics to "disappear" when you leave the shop.

I disagree.  If a mimic is mimicing something, say a chest, and the hero
sees it, then the hero remembers seeing a chest there.  That memory will
persist even when the mimic is out of sight, therefore the chest doesn't
disappear.  What we have is a monster imitating an object...


>Besides the obvious internal consistency,...

The inconsistancy is only in how you look at things.  If the screen consists
of what the hero sees and what the hero remembers, then there is no problem.
What the hero sees overrides what the hero remembers.  Once seen, however,
everything but monsters are remembered.  In the case of mimics, they are
imitating an object, hence they fall under the object rules.


